[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":95},"routes/item",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10},"item",{"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":18,"_19":-5,"_20":21,"_22":23,"_24":25,"_26":27,"_28":27,"_29":30,"_31":32,"_33":34,"_35":36,"_37":38,"_39":-5,"_40":-5,"_41":42,"_43":44,"_45":46,"_47":48,"_49":50,"_51":52,"_53":36,"_54":55,"_56":-5,"_57":36,"_58":59},"expiresAt",1794876035000,"id","w4dy2vcsnj6spz6135uq9wyzu","title","Google GTIG 披露 AI 智能体自动化漏洞利用与凭据盗用","originalTitle","Google GTIG Warns AI Agents Automate Exploits, Steal Credentials and Scale Cybercrime","summary","Google GTIG 观察到攻击者使用 ARTEX、CAIRN、CyberStrikeAI 和 PentAGI 等自主多智能体框架，自动完成漏洞发现、Payload 生成和多阶段入侵，其中 CAIRN 与 PentAGI 已用于真实攻击。","reason","source",{"_67":94},"links",{"_59":93},"publishedAt","2026-11-16T19:42:48.000Z","discoveredAt","2026-11-16T21:01:20.898Z","timelineAt","category","activity","tags",[70,68,87,88,89,90,91,92],"score",70,"selected",false,"channel","news","story","x","readingMode","full","author","Pierluigi Paganini","body",{"_83":-5,"_59":84,"_85":-5,"_86":36},"outline",[77],"relatedStories",[],"topics",[60,61,62,63,64],"indexable","markdownAvailable",true,"group","hasTranslation","bodyLanguage","original",{"_65":75,"_67":76},{"_65":73,"_67":74},{"_65":71,"_67":72},{"_65":69,"_67":70},{"_65":66,"_67":68},"slug","ai-security","name","AI 安全","attack","攻击活动","ai-agent","AI Agent 安全","identity","身份与访问","google","Google / 谷歌",{"_11":78,"_79":80,"_81":82},"sec-1","text","Google finds attackers using autonomous AI agents to automate exploits, steal AI","level",2,"zh","<h2 id=\"sec-1\">Google finds attackers using autonomous AI agents to automate exploits, steal AI access and scale intrusions, often sacrificing stealth for speed.</h2><p>Google’s Threat Intelligence Group (GTIG) says attackers have moved past typing prompts into a chatbot. They now run autonomous, multi-agent frameworks such as <a href=\"https://securityaffairs.com/tag/artex\">ARTEX</a>, CAIRN, CyberStrikeAI and PentAGI to carry out multi-stage intrusions faster. </p><p><em>“Adversary tradecraft has continued to transition beyond manual prompting to deploying autonomous multi-agent offensive frameworks—such as <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/artex-and-friends-enter-agentic-offensive-frameworks\">ARTEX</a>, CAIRN, CyberStrikeAI, and PentAGI—to execute multi-stage intrusions at greater operational velocity.” reads the <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/agentic-threat-activity-crosses-a-significant-threshold\">GTIG report</a>. “Threat actors are increasingly leveraging LLMs and agentic frameworks to fully automate the reverse engineering of patches via differential analysis, allowing them to rapidly weaponize n-day vulnerabilities and scale exploitation pipelines against internet-facing servers without human intervention.”</em></p><figure><a href=\"https://i0.wp.com/securityaffairs.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/image-39.png?ssl=1\"><img width=\"1920\" height=\"1080\" src=\"/dailyinfo/media/19a0acf646158a31.webp\" srcset=\"/dailyinfo/media/0260c148458d3aaa.webp 720w, /dailyinfo/media/33ce07fdd1dfc384.webp 1200w, /dailyinfo/media/19a0acf646158a31.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1536px) 760px, (min-width: 1024px) calc(100vw - 524px), (min-width: 640px) 608px, calc(100vw - 32px)\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"AI agents LLM\"></a></figure><p>The basic attack methods have not changed. What has changed is how quickly attackers can use them. These tools still rely on stolen credentials and systems that have not been patched. Amazon reached the same conclusion after monitoring its own decoy systems: None of the techniques in these campaigns were new. The difference is that AI agents can combine these techniques and carry out several steps without stopping.</p><p>These frameworks have three main capabilities. They can find and exploit simple vulnerabilities, ask an LLM to create custom payloads when needed, and change their approach based on the results they get. GTIG observed CAIRN and PentAGI being used in real attacks, while CyberStrikeAI and HexStrike AI were still being tested. CAIRN allows different AI agents to work together and share information through a common workspace. GTIG found evidence of its use against several targets, including organizations in the US aerospace sector.</p><p>CyberStrikeAI connects commercial and open-weight AI models to more than 100 security tools. HexStrike AI gives LLM-based clients access to over 150 tools, managed by more than 12 specialized agents. PentAGI is designed for less experienced operators. Between late August and early October, one attacker used a modified version of the framework to systematically scan organizations across Asia.</p><figure><a href=\"https://i0.wp.com/securityaffairs.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/image-40.png?ssl=1\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"576\" src=\"/dailyinfo/media/8f8938706ab9d3b1.webp\" srcset=\"/dailyinfo/media/c7398a8ac9d6b912.webp 720w, /dailyinfo/media/e73a3fde9e1fac60.webp 1200w, /dailyinfo/media/8f8938706ab9d3b1.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1536px) 760px, (min-width: 1024px) calc(100vw - 524px), (min-width: 640px) 608px, calc(100vw - 32px)\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"\"></a></figure><p>A China-nexus group also appears to be building its own framework. Others use ZeroHollow, an inference service for exploit development and routing, and Agent Zero, an open source agent framework that runs code on its own. Google expects the trend to continue, since the barrier to entry is low and adding exploits is easy.</p><p><em>“We have observed several threat actors separately experimenting with the use of these frameworks and harnesses, including financially motivated, as well as suspected espionage threat actors with a PRC nexus. This includes evidence that a PRC-nexus group was developing its own automated attack framework for likely use in cyber espionage operations.” continues the report. “These observations additionally extend to the use of other tools and services, such as ZeroHollow, an inference service used for exploit development and LLM routing, and Agent Zero, an open source agent framework which features autonomous code execution and dynamic tool generation.”</em></p><p>Vulnerability research is moving the same way. For the first time, GTIG saw attackers automate discovery and exploitation with no human in the loop. An exposed staging server hosted a framework called exploit_agent that compares popular web software before and after patches, drafts exploits and runs them. Victim data suggested interest in French media, entertainment and education sites, and the configuration notes were in Russian and told the operator to put the API keys in the file, not in the chat.</p><p>Once a vulnerability is patched, attackers have more information to work with. They can study the affected code, release notes, security research and proof-of-concept exploits to find ways to attack systems that have not yet been updated. Between January and August 2026, the number of vulnerability disclosures and exploits nearly doubled. Zero-day exploits also increased, from an average of 8 per month in 2025 to 11 in 2026, before reaching 22 in August.</p><p>The PaperCut case shows how quickly attackers can move. Two flaws were exploited as zero-days in late August, and Huntress and other researchers developed working exploits within 24 hours. GreyNoise <a href=\"https://www.greynoise.io/blog/ai-orchestrated-campaign-against-papercut-ng-mf\">detected</a> an attack campaign just four days after the flaws were disclosed. In one case, attackers went from gaining access to obtaining domain administrator privileges within minutes.</p><p>The same pattern appeared with two PHP vulnerabilities, CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619. Researchers used LLMs to analyse the flaws, and details about the patched code and a proof-of-concept video appeared before the October 21 patch. Hundreds of exploit claims then emerged, but many did not work. Defenders had to spend time checking which reports were genuine.</p><p>LLMjacking is the money side. Attackers steal LLM credentials and cloud resources, then resell access. Mandiant handled incidents where fraudulent charges passed $100,000 in a day, and the worst reached $1 million before cleanup.</p><p>They got in with credentials from public GitHub repositories and fake sites that harvest session tokens. One actor used CVE-2026-40217, a LiteLLM sandbox escape that turns limited keys into admin credentials. Some created and deleted short-lived service accounts to dodge rate limits, and others started powerful GPU instances. In one case two attackers used the same credentials, and a botnet made millions of API calls in about 24 hours before they were revoked.</p><p>The loot goes to “LLM mixers,” storefronts that route buyers through pools of stolen credentials and compute. </p><p><em>“Threat actors frequently monetize LLMJacking intrusions by selling access to compromised compute and LLM resources. In addition to the rise of LLMJacking, GTIG is tracking the proliferation of “LLM Mixers”—illicit storefronts that provide buyers with unauthorized access to LLM services and underlying proxy infrastructure.” continues the report. “This infrastructure routes buyer traffic through a pool of stolen credentials and compute instances to ensure reliable access, while also offering the ability to spread query volume across multiple victim environments to reduce the likelihood of detection.”</em></p><p>GTIG saw prices from $0.10 to $25 per million tokens on Russian- and Chinese-language markets. APIMart multiplies prices by five above 100,000 tokens, and JetNight sells monthly subscriptions. Even criminals have a surcharge for long prompts.</p><p>Information operations now use LLMs as standard tools, and GTIG says so with high confidence. That covers state groups tied to Russia, China and Iran, commercial influence vendors and profit-driven networks pumping out political clickbait. Better content doesn’t automatically mean more impact, and profit networks can look like state campaigns.</p><p>In August, GTIG found a platform built on the Gemini API and other tools to automate personas, media, posting and distribution. Prompts asked for local slang and deliberate typos, grainy phone-style photos with no background text, and residential proxies with saved login cookies. Another vendor was building a platform for over 100,000 accounts using cloud phone farms and AI-simulated behavior to age new accounts, with plans to sell to its own government and foreign clients.</p><p>Nation-state actors use LLMs for productivity. The Iranian actor <a href=\"https://securityaffairs.com/167095/security/google-disrupted-apt48-hacking-campaign.html\">CALANQUE ION</a>, formerly <a href=\"https://securityaffairs.com/tag/apt42\">APT42</a>, uses them for reconnaissance and social engineering, and Chinese actors use them for research, scanning and exploits. LEAKY CASTLE analyzes stolen data with them, others stage and exfiltrate through LLM platform file hosting, and North Korean IT workers use them for resumes and interviews.</p><p>MIDNIGHT NEPTUNE shows the creative end. Fake recruiter profiles with SynthID watermarks led targets to <a href=\"https://securityaffairs.com/tag/clickfix\">ClickFix </a>lures, and the attackers then edited .claude/settings.json in internal repositories so that starting Claude Code downloaded the SOMBERMEME backdoor. They also named access tokens after Microsoft Copilot. The Jieyou phishing service added an LLM chatbot in June so low-skilled affiliates can edit phishing panels in plain language.</p><p>AI tools also have limitations that attackers must consider. They can generate too much noise, make up details and even select the wrong targets. Groups focused on espionage are therefore likely to use them with caution, while cybercrime groups may accept a lower success rate in exchange for reaching more victims. Google SecOps has released detection rules for ARTEX, and Mandiant has published a guide to help organizations secure their systems. Our own analysis makes a similar point: Many <a href=\"https://securityaffairs.com/203230/hacking/from-report-to-tested-detection-in-hours-the-artex-signature-pack.html\">ARTEX “indicators”</a> found on compromised systems are generated by the LLM or by the tools it uses, rather than being built into the framework.</p><p>This makes attacker behavior more useful to defenders than looking for a single string or signature. When these frameworks run autonomously without tight controls, they leave traces in network traffic, recognizable identifiers and the code they execute. Google’s assessment offers some room for defenders to act: these frameworks currently trade stealth for velocity.</p><p><strong>Follow me on Twitter:&nbsp;</strong><a href=\"https://twitter.com/securityaffairs\"><strong>@securityaffairs</strong></a><strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;</strong><a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/sec.affairs\"><strong>Facebook</strong></a><strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;</strong><a href=\"https://infosec.exchange/@securityaffairs\"><strong>Mastodon</strong></a><strong></strong></p><p><a href=\"http://www.linkedin.com/pub/pierluigi-paganini/b/742/559\"><strong>Pierluigi&nbsp;Paganini</strong></a><strong></strong></p><p><strong>(</strong><a href=\"http://securityaffairs.co/wordpress/\"><strong>SecurityAffairs</strong></a><strong>&nbsp;–&nbsp;hacking,&nbsp;AI Agents)</strong></p>","zhKind","complete","Agent 安全","漏洞利用","凭据","大模型","Google","AWS","https://securityaffairs.com/203260/hacking/google-gtig-warns-ai-agents-automate-exploits-steal-credentials-and-scale-cybercrime.html","Security Affairs",{"_96":97,"_98":-5},"changelogVersion","2026-10-01T08:00","release"]
